      "text": "Unit of measure for farm 田 land 土",
      "record_character": "里",
      "kind": "imported_excerpt"
    },
    {
      "id": "E2",
      "source": "dong_chinese",
      "field": "hanzi.etymology_notes[1]",
      "text": "A village (里) is an area of land (土) where people have fields (田). In simplified Chinese 里 is also used to mean \"inside\", while in traditional Chinese this meaning is written with a separate character 裡.",
      "record_character": "里",
      "kind": "imported_excerpt"
    },
    {
      "id": "E3",
      "source": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "field": "hanzi.etymology_notes[2]",
      "text": "居也。从田从土。凡里之屬皆从里。",
      "record_character": "里",
      "kind": "imported_excerpt"
    },
    {
      "id": "E4",
      "source": "wiktionary",
      "field": "hanzi.etymology_notes[3]",
      "text": "Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 田 (“field”) + 土 (“soil; earth”) – a measure of land and fields; mile; place.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *rwəʔ (“village”) (STEDT, under PTB *r/g-wa). Cognate with 丘 (OC *kʰʷɯ) \"hill, mound\" & 街 (OC *kreː, *kreː) \"road crossing, street\"; outsides Sinitic, cognate with Mizo khua (“village”), Lai khûa (“village, cosmos”), Burmese ရွာ (rwa, “village”); Schuessler (2007) also proposes cognacy with Tibetan ར་བ (ra ba, “fence, enclosure, wall, pen”) by pointing to a semantic parallel between English town and German Zaun (“fence”).\nAlternatively, possibly related to 理 (lǐ) \"to divide into equal sections\", yet Schuessler also noted that early Chinese villages were not systematically planned and platted.",
      "record_character": "里",
      "kind": "imported_excerpt"
    },
